---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ToolWorkbench from '../../components/ToolWorkbench.astro';
import ExplainerBand from '../../components/ExplainerBand.astro';
import { analyze, DEFAULT_CSS } from '../../lib/audit/model';
import { auditView } from '../../lib/audit/views';
import { renderToString } from '../../lib/view/h';
import '../../styles/stylesheet-audit.css';

const tool = await getEntry('tools', 'stylesheet-audit');
if (!tool) throw new Error('Missing tools/stylesheet-audit.md');
const result = analyze(DEFAULT_CSS);
if (!result.ok) throw new Error(result.error.message);
const output = auditView(result.value).map(renderToString).join('');
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  title="Stylesheet Audit: CSS specificity, layers and findings"
  heading="See the shape of your CSS"
  lead="Stylesheet Audit: follow specificity from the first selector to the last."
  app="stylesheet-audit"
>
  <ToolWorkbench inputLabel="CSS source">
    <Fragment slot="input">
      <p class="notice" data-audit-notice role="status" hidden></p>
      <div class="field">
        <label class="label" id="audit-input-label" for="audit-css">
          Your CSS
        </label>
        <textarea
          class="input audit-css"
          id="audit-css"
          rows="8"
          spellcheck="false"
          autocapitalize="off"
          autocomplete="off"
          aria-describedby="audit-help"
          data-audit-css
        >{DEFAULT_CSS}</textarea>
        <p class="input-help" id="audit-help">
          100 KB max; first 5,000 selectors. Share links: up to 8 KB. Byte limits use UTF-8. Individual
          selectors: 1,000 characters; nesting expansions: 2,000 characters; expanded layer paths: 100 KB. CSS
          is parsed locally, never applied. Imports and resources are never fetched. No analytics or input
          storage. Only Copy link puts CSS in a URL; links are not encrypted.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="duel-actions js-only">
        <button class="button" type="button" data-audit-copy aria-describedby="audit-copy-status">
          Copy link
        </button>
        <p class="status" id="audit-copy-status" data-audit-copy-status role="status"></p>
      </div>
      <div class="field" data-audit-link-wrap hidden>
        <label class="label" for="audit-link">
          Link to this audit
        </label>
        <input class="input" id="audit-link" type="text" readonly data-audit-link />
      </div>
      <p class="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-audit-live></p>
    </Fragment>
    <div slot="output" class="flow" data-audit-output set:html={output} />
  </ToolWorkbench>
  <ExplainerBand slot="explainer" labelledby="audit-explainer">
    <div class="prose flow">
      <h2 id="audit-explainer">A map, not a verdict</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        A specificity graph shows selector weight in source order. Peaks identify places to review, not
        guaranteed bugs. A higher-weight rule can resist later rules only when they compete in the same
        cascade context. Origin, importance and layers are considered before specificity. Read{' '}
        <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Cascade/Specificity">
          how specificity fits the cascade
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <h3>Read the full triple</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        Specificity compares ID, class and type columns from left to right. The graph caps each column at nine
        solely for display; sorting and findings use the full triple. A spike here means higher specificity
        than every available entry in the next ten positions, with at least one following entry. No
        stylesheet-wide score or grade is assigned.
      </p>
      <h3>Lower weight deliberately</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        <code>:where()</code> and its arguments add no specificity. <code>:is()</code>, <code>:not()</code>{' '}
        and <code>:has()</code> use their most specific argument. Nesting uses the maximum weight of the
        parent selector list, not necessarily the parent that matches. See{' '}
        <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/selectors-4/#specificity-rules">Selectors Level 4</a> and{' '}
        <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Nesting/Nesting_and_specificity">
          nesting and specificity
        </a>
        . Removing weight changes the cascade; check the rendered page before applying a suggestion.
      </p>
      <h3>Give layers a job</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        Declare a layer order before adding rules. Later layers outrank earlier ones for normal declarations
        within the same origin; unlayered normal declarations take priority over layered ones. The order
        reverses for important declarations. Within a parent layer, its own normal declarations outrank its
        sublayers. See{' '}
        <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@layer">
          the layer rules
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <h3>What a pasted stylesheet cannot tell us</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        This audit does not evaluate media conditions, match selectors against a document, expand imports, or
        compare property values. Keyframes, font descriptors and unsupported at-rule groups are not selector
        rules and are reported as excluded. Rules with declarations after nested rules appear as separate
        declaration runs to preserve source order. Empty rules remain visible. The report is a starting point
        for review, not a replacement for browser inspection.
      </p>
    </div>
  </ExplainerBand>
</ToolLayout>
<script>
  import '../../scripts/stylesheet-audit';
</script>
